#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "337be080-59df-51bf-b0d5-14dba139b9c0" :knowledge-point/title "Parametrizing a Horizontal Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f14d5ba4-85b6-520a-8734-6e7f0a153e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292098} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca0e2ed-49f6-5181-9312-902ef85eb1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292099} {:answer/id #uuid "bcc7d760-1a3e-55dd-8314-3c9b3f135582" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-5=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-2=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292100} {:answer/id #uuid "af9dc71d-e91f-5d2b-bf3e-298a5b0c44bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292101} {:answer/id #uuid "42a1e879-dc0a-5af0-be45-86a3eb7cbace" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292102}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f14d5ba4-85b6-520a-8734-6e7f0a153e0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292098} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c1f251a-b759-512b-aeed-c7833b5ff71d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b103e245-fa8b-566e-aedb-314d0caff13b" :question/math-academy-id "q-106464" :question/problem "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-5)^{2}=2(x-2)?$" :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,5).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-5=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p(x-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p & =2 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y-5=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-5=t.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Equation of Parabola Given Its Parametric Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ee9711b-2f58-5f63-8f70-bbcc2182287d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5)^{2}=-8(y-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292105} {:answer/id #uuid "c6416754-c8d9-5f89-8385-0eb9c969c480" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-5)^{2}=8(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292106} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcd83f9-d129-5bd3-b4b3-a0cb6de23969" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-5)^{2}=-8(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292107} {:answer/id #uuid "80ceae45-28dc-5749-a076-5b46dd832dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292108} {:answer/id #uuid "a4169520-377a-597f-a07d-944f9951066d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292109}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80ceae45-28dc-5749-a076-5b46dd832dfc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292108} :answer-field/id #uuid "0a00b481-bf85-58c8-b8ac-4381399f3886" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2830887f-a29d-5706-9c2e-a6b72884e503" :question/math-academy-id "q-106502" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=-4t,\\,y-5=-2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-2}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-2}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{y-5}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-8(y-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
